 dependent on its northern neighbor is  australia that it has often been  described only partly humorously as a  chinese satellite state  clearly believing this narrative china  tried to bully what it perceived as  small easy prey in 2020 over what it  called 14 grievances which include  australia's call for an investigation  into the origins of covet 19.  what followed was nothing short of a  full-blown trade war  china imposed tariffs duties and bans  not to mention a state-organized boycott  of australian imports  but beijing overplayed its hand while  the trade disruption wasn't without  costs australia turned around and found  new buyers  first china began looking for new  sellers to replace australia  those turned out to be russia and  indonesia  russia and indonesia previously supplied  india japan and south korea who were  likewise now in need of a new supplier  as you can see here their new supplier  was australia  think of trade like a city's road  network  sure you can block off one street but  traffic will simply find a new route  any disruption will ripple throughout  the city causing some congestion but a  new equilibrium will eventually be found  bans tariffs trade wars and sanctions  therefore aren't necessarily as  catastrophic as they may seem  it all depends on what is being traded  australia was selling iron coal and beef  high demand commodities for which there  are many possible buyers  likewise although oil iron soybeans and  copper are among china's largest imports  by value we can safely assume it will  always have access to a seller  and not just because these are  commodities  in the wake of u.s sanctions against  russia many were surprised by the degree  of international cooperation  even historically neutral countries like  switzerland and singapore proactively  complied and publicly denounced the war  if china were to say seize and occupy an  outlying taiwanese island it would  prompt similar reflection from fence  sitters like singapore  but getting everyone to cooperate with  the toughest kind of sanctions against  china would be a much more difficult  challenge  iron soybeans and oil would likely keep  flowing from brazil for example  the country simply has too much to lose  if push came to shove china could roll  back some of its environmental  regulations allowing it to more easily  source dirty commodities like coal  domestically  the catch  everything would be more expensive  a lot more expensive  no country has benefited more from the  us navy's guarding of the world shipping  lanes than china  if it were ever to withdraw that  protection china would enter a world of  hurt  but that's the easy stuff  the real challenge would be advanced  technology  you don't have to take my word for it  either  china has assembled what is effectively  a list of its own weaknesses  technologies and components it wants to  be able to produce domestically  despite investing many tens of billions  of dollars into commercial airplane  manufacturing for example china is  almost entirely dependent on foreign  imports  its most commercially viable domestic  plane the c919 still requires parts from  ge an american company  tellingly no one outside of china buys  it  if the u.s ever applied maximum  sanctions chinese commercial aviation  would simply crash and burn though its  fast network of high-speed rail would  somewhat soften the blow  at the very least the industry would be  forced to regress something like 40  years  but even this hypothetical bloodbath  pales in comparison to semiconductors  there's a reason they receive so much  air time and discussions about china the  country buys a ton of them  in purely dollar terms it imports as  many semiconductors as it does crude oil  and that's saying a lot china buys  one-fourth of the entire world's oil by  value  even after investing 22 billion dollars  on a national semiconductor fund in 2014  and plenty more since the truth is that  china remains years behind